course('risk', 'Risk management and psychology', 'fa-scale-balanced', 'Position sizing, risk-reward, drawdowns, the trading plan, journaling and the mindset.', [
    t('position-sizing', 'Position sizing: the 1% rule', ['Risk 0.5–1% of the account per trade.', 'Lots = money at risk ÷ (stop distance × value per pip).',
                                                          'Wider stop → smaller lots.'],
      ["Professional traders decide <b>how much they can lose</b> before they decide how much they can win. With 1% risk, ten losses in a row cost about 10% of the account, which is recoverable.",
       "Example: $1,000 account, 1% = $10. Stop 20 pips on EURUSD (about $1 per pip per 0.1 lot): $10 ÷ 20 pips = $0.50 per pip = 0.05 lots. The risk calculator in every PATREOAIX signal does this maths for you."]),
    t('risk-reward', 'Risk-to-reward and win rate', ['R:R = target distance ÷ stop distance.', 'At 1:2 you are profitable with a 35% win rate.',
                                                     'Think in R: +2R, −1R.'],
      ["If you risk 20 pips to make 40, your <b>risk-to-reward</b> is 1:2. Your required win rate falls as R:R rises: at 1:1 you need over 50% winners; at 1:2 about 34%; at 1:3 about 25%.",
       "Measure results in <b>R</b> (multiples of the risk): it makes progress comparable no matter the lot size. The PATREOAIX journal and signal results use R."]),
    t('drawdown', 'Drawdowns and how to survive them', ['Losing streaks are normal: 6–8 in a row happens.', 'A 50% loss needs a 100% gain to recover.',
                                                        'Cut size after a series of losses.'],
      ["Even a good strategy has losing streaks. With a 50% win rate, 7 losses in a row will happen over a few hundred trades.",
       "Losses are asymmetric: −10% needs +11% to recover, −50% needs +100%. Protect the account first: reduce risk to 0.5% after a 5% drawdown, stop trading for the day after 2–3 losses."]),
    t('trading-plan', 'Writing a trading plan', ['Markets, timeframes and sessions you trade.', 'Exact entry rules and the setups you take.',
                                                 'Risk per trade, daily loss limit.', 'When you do NOT trade.'],
      ["A <b>trading plan</b> turns trading into a repeatable process. Write down: which markets and sessions, your setups (e.g. pullback to demand + engulfing), stop and target rules, risk per trade, a maximum daily loss, and rules for news.",
       "If a trade doesn't match the plan, it isn't a trade. Review the plan monthly using your journal."]),
    t('journaling', 'Journaling and review', ['Record every trade with a screenshot and the reason.', 'Review weekly: what worked, what to stop.',
                                              'Track your results in R by setup.'],
      ["The fastest way to improve is a <b>journal</b>. For each trade note the setup, the entry reason, the emotions, and the outcome in R. Add a screenshot of the chart.",
       "Every week look for patterns: which setups pay, which sessions lose, which mistakes repeat. PATREOAIX logs your trades automatically and its AI coach reviews your week every Monday."]),
    t('psychology', 'Trading psychology: fear, greed, FOMO, revenge', ['FOMO: chasing a move you missed.', 'Revenge trading: trying to win back a loss immediately.',
                                                                         'Overtrading: too many trades from boredom.', 'Fix: rules, small size and breaks.'],
      ["Most traders don't fail because of analysis; they fail because of behaviour. <b>FOMO</b> makes you chase price far from a good entry. <b>Revenge trading</b> doubles the size after a loss. <b>Fear</b> makes you close winners too early and hold losers too long.",
       "The cure is structure: a written plan, fixed 1% risk, stopping after a daily loss limit, and stepping away from the screen after a big win or loss."]),
])

